  "text": "⒡\n\n⒡\n\n最ധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധधധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധधധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധധधധधധധധധധधधधധधധध\nTo find the maximum possible length of the fourth altitude of the tetrahedron with four different altitudes and three altitudes measuring \\(\\sqrt{80}\\) units each, we need to consider the properties of the tetrahedron and the altitudes.\n\nLet's denote the tetrahedron by \\(V_1, V_2, V_3, V_4\\). The altitudes corresponding to the vertices \\(V_1, V_2, V_3\\) are \\(\\sqrt{80}\\) units each. Let's denote the fourth vertex by \\(V_4\\) and its altitude against \\(V_4\\) by \\(h_4\\).\n\nFirst, note that the volume \\(V\\) of the tetrahedron can be expressed in terms of the altitudes and the areas of the faces. For a tetrahedron with vertices having integer coordinates, it is known that the volume is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) times the determinant of a matrix formed by three vectors in one of the faces.\n\nGiven that the three altitudes measured from four different vertices are \\(\\sqrt{80}\\), we can use the fact that the volume of a tetrahedron with integer coordinates is a rational number, and the area of the face opposite to the vertex with altitude \\(\\sqrt{80}\\) is a rational number times the area of the face opposite to another vertex.\n\nThe maximum possible length of the fourth altitude \\(h_4\\) is when it is as large as possible. This happens when the tetrahedron is maximally stretched, i.e., it almost divides to a plane. This would mean that the fourth altitude would be as large as possible relative to the other three altitudes. The maximum possible value of the fourth altitude for a tetrahedron with three altitudes \\(\\sqrt{80}\\) is constrained by the requirement that the volume must be rational, and the altitudes must be rational multiples of the same square root.\n\nUsing the maximum rationality constraint, the fourth altitude \\(h_4\\) must be a rational number multiplied by \\(\\sqrt{80}\\). The maximum value of \\(h_4\\) occurs when \\(h_4\\) is as large as allowed under the rationality constraint and the requirement that the volume and areas be rational.\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible length of the fourth altitude \\(h_4\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
